How much junk mail do you get ? I used to be deluged with the stuff
until I took steps to recreate some privacy for myself. While,
obviously, not being hassled by anyone who can afford to buy a mailing
list is not as important to me as not being shot by the cops would be
to a criminal, there are good reasons to desire privacy regardless of
whether or not you have a 'government-approved' lifestyle (not that
many people do these days in any case).

> As for convenience, I'm not sure if digicash has tremendous advantages
> over credit cards

- Fraud ? If someone gets your card number they can bill anything to you,
and while you'll probably be able to avoid paying for it, it will still
probably involve a lot of hassle for you. With digicash, of course, you
can be stung for the whole cost of the transaction, but that would have
a bad effect on the reputation of the seller, and for the purchase of
data you can probably arrange protocols to ensure that you only would
only lose a small amount (e.g. something like buying $ 100 worth of
data in 100 chunks for $ 1 each). Hmm, I don't think either system has
an obvious advantage from this point of view.

> --it seems as though what people mostly want from
> credit cards (and are willing to pay quite a bit for) is credit--

I don't think so, at least in the UK. From what I remember most people
just use them for payment and pay the bill in full at the end of the
month. Although I do have an outstanding balance on my cards at the
moment I mostly use them merely to delay bills for a month or to pay
large bills that I can't easily cover with cash or check. It's rare
that I deliberately use them for long-term credit (the interest rates
are way too high for that).

> Does anyone have any thought about how a transition from digicash
> based on fiat money to an independent digicash system would work?
> I'm assuming that the federal government will be weakened enough
> that such a transition is very likely.

Personally, I suspect that it will start small with numerous localised
(in cyberspace I mean, not geographically localised) digicash systems,
and after a while they'll begin to trade/exchange cash with each other
until the government monetary systems simply become irrelevant, or the
governments stick control electrodes in all our brains...

		Mark

