Subject: [#94-6-75] SCI: Chaos, Complexity, and Reductionism

At 11:40 AM 6/13/94 -0800, Doug Cutrell wrote:
>strictly local rules give rise to information carrying "particles" which
>propagate through the array with varying rates, and interact with each
>other when they collide.  [...]Different computational strategies discovered
>by different CA can be completely analyzed by an identification of the
>types of particles that can evolve, their directions and speeds of
>propagation, and the rules for interaction that describe what happens when
>two particles collide (...)  It is like a kind of "particle physics" in
>the "active media" of the cellular array.

Yes, this is what I wanted to think of as emergence. But the post doesn't
clear up the controversy. I think the reductionists want to say, "there's
nothing emergent about it, all that behaviour is implicit in the CA rules
and if we were smart enough we could just look at the rules and infer all
that." And there's something to that - very little in nature builds order
out of some global control mechanism the way humans like to do it.

What really is the distinction we want to make with this word? The
components of the system interact locally, yet support a global order. ?
But all the parts of an automobile interact locally, supporting a global
order, yet I wouldn't want to call cars an emergent property of auto parts.
Maybe self-organization?

