From: Mark Grant <Mark.Grant@isltd.insignia.com>
Date: Sun, 19 Jun 94 20:24:27 BST
Subject: [#94-6-77] digital cash and free banking

-----BEGIN PGP SIGNED MESSAGE-----

nancy@genie.slhs.udel.edu said :

> How can a seller tell whether a user of digicash is legitimate? I thought
> that the whole point of digicash was that the user couldn't be
> traced back to a particular person. 

It depends on the system. The seller can always tell if it's legitimate
digicash because it will be signed by the bank and noone else can forge
it unless they either have a copy of the bank's key or know a secret
method of breaking the signature algorithm. The algorithm for creating
the cash prevents the bank from linking the buyer and seller (unless
the buyer is in collusion with the bank), because the digicash that it
gets back from the seller will be different to the digicash that it
sent out to the buyer, and the buyer and seller can if they wish use
remailers, PO boxes, etc to hide their identities from each other.

Even when they're completely anonymous, the only way for the buyer to
defraud the seller is to give them digicash that's already been spent.
The simple system requires the seller to send the digicash to the bank
as soon as they receive it, and then the bank will tell them whether
it's already been spent or not.  Some systems allow the seller to
verify the signature on the cash, then request additional, verifiable,
information from the buyer that will tell them nothing about the
identity of the buyer if the cash is only spent once, but can be used
by the bank, when the seller eventually forwards the cash to it, to
identify the buyer if it is spent more than once. Another system is
supposed to work just like paper money, but I don't know how that works
at a low level.

				Mark

