From: pcm@world.std.com (Peter C. McCluskey)
Date: Sun, 19 Jun 1994 22:39:59 -0400
Subject: [#94-6-80] digital cash and free banking

 jbaker@halcyon.com  (James Baker) writes in X-Message-Number: #94-6-72:
>There are those who say the central bank (Federal Reserve in the US) is
>irrevelant anyway; with or without digital cash. Even some in the Federal
>Reserve banks say this, and the calculations based on M2 and M3 sort of
>bear this out.

 The Fed dosn't have full control over M2/M3, but that hardly makes it
irrelevant. Do you mean to say that nothing the Fed does to the monetary
base matters? Think about the effects of 100%/year growth.

>1. Fixed value. Digital cash could be defined to have no inflation or
>deflation. Fixed value becomes even more valuable for international
>transactions by elliminating exchange rate risk.

 Metallic and fiat currencies have been "defined" to have zero inflation.
That hasn't stopped their values from changing. The value of any freely
traded entity is determined by supply and demand, not by any definition.
You could make the value of the currency fixed relative to some commodity
or basket of commodities by making that currency amount to ownership of
the commodities, but no fixed basket of commodities can perfectly reflect
the changing set of goods that people actually buy.

>4. Credit. If people and businesses could in effect issue their own
>currency and it's value be determined by others' perception of it's value
>(like corporate credit but with less overhead) I think we would get much
>better loans made and less credit risk overall.  Today it would reveal too
>much information about ourselves to get a credit rating from everyone we
>wish to do business with, plus it would be time consuming. 

 Credit and cash are independent concepts. Unless you are merely saying that
some of the code used to handle digicash would also be useful for handling
credit, I have missed your point.

>5. Tax collection. Huh? Yes, tax collection. Say your digital cash
>card/computer had a resident tax agent program running inside. Each
>transaction would be monitored by the program and at the end of the quarter
>the tax agent program would issue a figure signed by it's own digital
>signature. That's all the info you give the IRS; the amount and the
>signature. Even your identity could be kept secret if digital cash was used
>on a wide enough scale. (I'll explain this later if anyone is interrested.)
>It would be incumbant upon the taxing authorities to write tax agent
>programs  popular enough to gain credibility in the marketplace. 

 Making tax agents popular/credible won't convince people to use them.
The biggest question is whether the IRS will be able to monitor digicash
transactions. If it can't then digicash will put the IRS out of business
(which is why the Fed's control over the money supply will not be a big
concern to the government). If the IRS can monitor transactions then lots
of people will write tax agents, the same way they write software to file
tax forms now. But it it hard to forsee a fully automated tax calculation
system, because people want different kinds of transactions treated
differently for tax purposes (i.e. you probably don't want gifts that
you receive to be taxed as income, and you may want charitable donations
you make deducted from your income). The IRS can't seem to come up with
a set of rules for classifying transactions that are coherent enough for
its agents to understand.

