From: Eric Watt Forste <arkuat@netcom.com>
Date: Mon, 20 Jun 94 00:23:06 -0700
Subject: [#94-6-82] digital cash and free banking 

>> Is there really any relationship?

Only with the suppression of free banking is it possible to sever the 
connection between what we currently call money and "commodity money". 
Gold is only one possible commodity money.

Again and again, I have heard people ask "what is it that backs
digital cash up? what is it that gives digital cash its value?" The
answer is, the same thing that gave cash (banknotes) its value when it
was first invented: valuable commmodities on deposit.

An understanding of free banking is essential to the conduct of 
institutions which accept deposits of valuable commodities in exchange
for valuable digital-cash "banknotes".

If we back digital cash with FRNs, the game is half-conceded. Not to
mention that the regs that prohibit us  from backing digital cash 
with FRNs are even more ferocious than the regs that prohibit us
from backing digital cash with barterable goods (e. g. gold, which is 
only *one* possibility among many).

